@899. "Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from henceforth" = the resurrection into eternal life of those who have lived the life of charity hitherto, and will live it henceforth.

@--[3]. The reason "death" = resurrection, and that therefore "the dead" = those who rise again to eternal life, is that "death" = Hell, and consequently evils and falsities; and these must die in order that man may receive spiritual life; for before these are dead and extinguished man has not spiritual life... wherefore, by "dying" here and elsewhere in the Word, is meant the extinction of man's Own life... Moreover, by "dying" in the spiritual sense may be meant resurrection... Natural death, too, is nothing but resurrection...

@--[5]. Hence it is evident, that "death" = both spiritual death, which is damnation, and also resurrection into life, which is salvation. Illustrated.

