@324. Man is able... thus to receive the Divine; and he who is able to receive the Divine, so as to see and perceive it in himself, cannot but be conjoined with the Lord, and by this conjunction live to eternity. What would the Lord [do] with the whole creation of the universe, unless He had created images and likenesses of Himself, to whom He could communicate His Divine?... What would there be Divine in these things, unless they were for the sake of the end that they might be of service to subjects which might receive the Divine more nearly, and might see and feel it? And as the Divine is of glory inexhaustible, would He keep it to Himself, and could He do so?

